The prose claims that $\hat\Xi_t$ is a consistent covariance estimator for conditioned SGD and that Theorem sec4:thm1 does not require $B_t$ to converge to the true Hessian, making the analysis directly applicable. This is contradicted by the paper's own development: Theorem sec4:thm1 is stated under Assumption 3, whose unbiased-Hessian condition forces $B_t	o B^\star=
abla^2F( x^\star)$ (Lemma sec4:lem1, Part 2), and the consistency proof relies essentially on this convergence -- the approximation error $\hat theta^k$ is controlled by $\mE\|B_k-B^\star\|^2$ (Lemma appen:A4:lem5) and the bias term $ delta^i$ contains $\{B_i^{-1}-(B^\star)^{-1}\}
abla F_i$, which vanishes only when $B_t	o B^\star$. For general preconditioners (AdaGrad, RMSProp, quasi-Newton) Assumption 3 fails, the target $\Xi^\star$ built from $B^\star$ is not the method's limiting covariance, the linearization $B_t^{-1}
abla F_t\approx x_t- x^\star$ underlying the decomposition of Lemma appen:A4:lem1 breaks (the preconditioned dynamics involve the generally nonsymmetric matrix $ ar B^{-1}
abla^2F( x^\star)$), and the symmetric diagonalization $I-C^\star=U\Sigma U^T$ used throughout has no analogue. No result in the paper covers conditioned SGD.
